WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.200
Here is the cast. The pentoses ribose and deoxyribose build your nucleic acids; the

2
00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:11.300
only difference is one missing hydroxyl.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:16.700
The hexoses glucose, mannose, and galactose differ at just one carbon each mannose

4
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.440
at C2, galactose at C4 which makes them epimers of glucose.

5
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:26.020
And fructose, off to the side, is the odd one: a ketose, with its carbonyl at carbon

6
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:29.640
two. Six sugars carry most of the biological traffic.

7
00:00:30.683 --> 00:00:32.323
Why don't sugars stay straight?

8
00:00:32.743 --> 00:00:37.323
The chemistry is simple: an aldehyde reacts with an alcohol to form a hemiacetal,

9
00:00:37.323 --> 00:00:39.983
and a ketone with an alcohol to form a hemiketal.

10
00:00:40.683 --> 00:00:45.923
In an aldohexose like glucose, the sugar's own hydroxyl on carbon five swings around

11
00:00:45.923 --> 00:00:51.163
and attacks its carbonyl at carbon one, closing the molecule into a ring no enzyme

12
00:00:51.163 --> 00:00:53.143
needed, like a self-closing gate.

13
00:00:53.763 --> 00:00:56.383
That ring-closure is what creates the anomeric carbon.

14
00:00:57.433 --> 00:00:58.813
Watch glucose close.

15
00:00:59.493 --> 00:01:03.493
The open-chain aldehyde folds so the carbon-five hydroxyl reaches the carbonyl at

16
00:01:03.493 --> 00:01:05.653
carbon one, and the ring snaps shut.

17
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:10.913
The old carbonyl carbon becomes a brand-new chiral center, the anomeric carbon, and

18
00:01:10.913 --> 00:01:16.013
it can point two ways: the alpha form has the C1 hydroxyl below the ring, the beta

19
00:01:16.013 --> 00:01:20.993
form above. In solution glucose interconverts between them through the open chain,

20
00:01:21.413 --> 00:01:24.033
settling near one-third alpha and two-thirds beta.

21
00:01:25.133 --> 00:01:27.113
This is the whole chapter on one slide.

22
00:01:27.713 --> 00:01:29.373
Take identical glucose monomers.

23
00:01:29.973 --> 00:01:34.393
Link them with α-1,4 bonds and the chain coils into a compact, digestible storage

24
00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:37.313
polymer: — starch in plants, glycogen in animals.

25
00:01:38.053 --> 00:01:42.573
Link those monomers with β-1,4 bonds and the chains straighten, hydrogen-bond into

26
00:01:42.573 --> 00:01:46.033
rigid cellulose fibers, which your own enzymes cannot digest.

27
00:01:46.753 --> 00:01:49.133
One bond geometry, opposite destinies.

28
00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:53.733
Your pencil's cellulose and your blood glucose use the same brick, laid a different

29
00:01:53.733 --> 00:01:54.733
way.

30
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:56.887
Glycogen's branching is the point.

31
00:01:57.727 --> 00:02:02.247
α-1,4 chains run the length, and an α-1,6 branch sprouts about every ten glucose

32
00:02:02.247 --> 00:02:08.387
units. A full-size model particle holds 55,000 glucose units, with about 2,000 outer

33
00:02:08.387 --> 00:02:10.047
ends. Why does that matter?

34
00:02:10.607 --> 00:02:14.927
Glycogen phosphorylase works at those ends, letting many enzyme molecules act at

35
00:02:14.927 --> 00:02:20.167
once. Branching is parallel processing for energy: your sprinting muscles tap their

36
00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:21.187
own glycogen fast.
